   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Agreement Between Handheld and Fixed Dynamometry for Hip Strength Assessment in Young Soccer Players and Non-Soccer Controls: An Exploratory Study</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Agreement Between Handheld and Fixed Dynamometry for Hip Strength Assessment in Young Soccer Players and Non-Soccer Controls: An Exploratory Study</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Bland--Altman analysis; DynaMo; fixed dynamometry; ForceFrame; handheld dynamometry; hip muscle strength; method agreement; soccer</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Background/Objectives: Handheld dynamometers are increasingly used for muscle strength assessment because of their low cost</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Background/Objectives: Handheld dynamometers are increasingly used for muscle strength assessment because of their low cost</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">portability</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">portability</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and ease of implementation in clinical and sports settings. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and ease of implementation in clinical and sports settings. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">it remains unclear whether measurements obtained using handheld and externally fixed dynamometers can be used interchangeably in young soccer players. This exploratory cross-sectional study compared measurements of hip adductor and abductor strength obtained using a handheld dynamometer (DynaMo) and an externally fixed dynamometer (ForceFrame) in young soccer players and non-soccer controls. Methods: Forty healthy men (20 U-21 soccer players and 20 non-soccer controls) were included. Isometric hip adduction and abduction strength were assessed bilaterally using DynaMo and ForceFrame during a single testing session. Agreement between measurement methods was evaluated using Bland--Altman analysis</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">it remains unclear whether measurements obtained using handheld and externally fixed dynamometers can be used interchangeably in young soccer players. This exploratory cross-sectional study compared measurements of hip adductor and abductor strength obtained using a handheld dynamometer (DynaMo) and an externally fixed dynamometer (ForceFrame) in young soccer players and non-soccer controls. Methods: Forty healthy men (20 U-21 soccer players and 20 non-soccer controls) were included. Isometric hip adduction and abduction strength were assessed bilaterally using DynaMo and ForceFrame during a single testing session. Agreement between measurement methods was evaluated using Bland--Altman analysis</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and associations between methods were determined using Spearmanâ€™s rank correlation coefficients. Results: Moderate to very strong positive correlations were observed between DynaMo and ForceFrame measurements (r = 0.44--0.96)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and associations between methods were determined using Spearmanâ€™s rank correlation coefficients. Results: Moderate to very strong positive correlations were observed between DynaMo and ForceFrame measurements (r = 0.44--0.96)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">although the correlation for left hip abduction in soccer players did not reach statistical significance (p = 0.052). Nevertheless</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">although the correlation for left hip abduction in soccer players did not reach statistical significance (p = 0.052). Nevertheless</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Bland--Altman analysis revealed a systematic bias</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Bland--Altman analysis revealed a systematic bias</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with ForceFrame producing higher normalized hip strength values than DynaMo across all assessments. The mean bias ranged from 2.02 to 3.34 N/kg (95% LoA 0.21 to 4.83 N/kg) in the soccer group</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with ForceFrame producing higher normalized hip strength values than DynaMo across all assessments. The mean bias ranged from 2.02 to 3.34 N/kg (95% LoA 0.21 to 4.83 N/kg) in the soccer group</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and 0.92 to 2.00 N/kg (95% LoA 0.18 to 2.93 N/kg) in the non-soccer group. Conclusions: Although moderate to very strong correlations were observed between the two dynamometry systems</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and 0.92 to 2.00 N/kg (95% LoA 0.18 to 2.93 N/kg) in the non-soccer group. Conclusions: Although moderate to very strong correlations were observed between the two dynamometry systems</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">systematic differences in recorded strength values indicate that the two systems may not be considered directly interchangeable for assessing hip abductor and adductor strength. For longitudinal monitoring and clinical decision-making</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">systematic differences in recorded strength values indicate that the two systems may not be considered directly interchangeable for assessing hip abductor and adductor strength. For longitudinal monitoring and clinical decision-making</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">repeated assessments should preferably be performed using the same measurement system</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">repeated assessments should preferably be performed using the same measurement system</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and caution is warranted when interpreting absolute hip muscle strength values. Given the exploratory design and relatively small sample</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and caution is warranted when interpreting absolute hip muscle strength values. Given the exploratory design and relatively small sample</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">these findings should be interpreted cautiously and confirmed in larger studies.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">these findings should be interpreted cautiously and confirmed in larger studies.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
